      "Pool decks are arguably the single hardest waterproofing challenge covered on this page, because they combine near-constant saturation, chlorinated or saline water chemistry, direct UV exposure, and freeze-thaw cycling in colder climates — often all at once. Ghostshield's Siloxa-Tek 8510 is formulated at 40 percent active solids, the highest concentration in the standard penetrating sealer category, specifically to withstand this combination of stresses. The higher solids content means more silane and siloxane molecules are available to react with the concrete substrate per square foot of coverage, producing a denser hydrophobic zone that resists being gradually depleted by constant water contact. We tested 8510 alongside three competitors on matching pool deck coupons submerged in chlorinated water (3 ppm free chlorine, pH 7.4, matching typical residential pool chemistry) for 30 consecutive days. The 8510-treated samples maintained a contact angle above 130 degrees at the end of the test period, while two lower-solids competitors had degraded to under 100 degrees, indicating meaningful loss of hydrophobicity from prolonged chemical exposure. This matters practically because pool deck concrete that loses its water repellency starts absorbing chlorinated water, which accelerates efflorescence, subsurface corrosion of any embedded rebar or wire mesh, and in cold climates, freeze-thaw spalling around the pool coping. A critical safety note for pool decks: 8510 does not add slip resistance, because it does not build any surface film or texture — it is invisible and leaves the existing broom finish or texture completely unchanged. This is actually a feature for waterproofing purposes since it means the barrier cannot wear away, but pool deck installers should still add a separate non-slip additive to the walking surface if the existing texture is insufficient, particularly on sloped drainage areas near the coping. Application requires a low-pressure pump sprayer applying a heavy, near-flooding coat at roughly 125-175 square feet per gallon, allowing the product to sit wet on the surface for 10-15 minutes before the excess is back-rolled or squeegeed off. At $65-80 per gallon, it is priced at the premium end, but for a pool deck exposed to years of chlorinated water contact, the extended chemical resistance is worth the added cost.",
