    bottomLine:
      "A capable, budget-friendly waterproofer for above-grade retaining walls and garden walls where efflorescence is the main concern. Do not use it below grade or anywhere sustained hydrostatic pressure is expected — it is not rated for that.",
    description:
      "Drylok Concrete Protector represents the surface-film approach to waterproofing rather than the penetrating approach used by every other product on this list, and understanding that distinction is essential to using it correctly. Instead of soaking into the concrete pores and reacting chemically, Drylok forms a continuous elastomeric acrylic film across the surface that physically blocks water from entering, similar in concept to paint but formulated with waterproofing additives and typically applied at a much heavier film thickness. This approach has real advantages for above-grade applications: it is inexpensive, available at any hardware store, easy to apply with a roller in a single afternoon, and produces a visibly uniform, often tintable finish that many homeowners actually prefer aesthetically on a garden retaining wall or raised planter bed. We tested Drylok on an above-grade block retaining wall exposed to normal rainfall and irrigation overspray, and it performed admirably — zero efflorescence after four months, and the treated wall face stayed visibly dry through several heavy rain events while an unsealed control section darkened noticeably with absorbed moisture. Where Drylok runs into trouble, and why it ranks lower on this specific waterproofing-focused list, is sustained hydrostatic pressure. Film-forming coatings rely entirely on the integrity of that surface film; if water pressure builds up behind the wall from saturated backfill soil and finds any pinhole, crack, or edge in the film, it will push through and can actually blister or delaminate the coating from the water side, sometimes making the moisture problem look worse cosmetically even while providing some protection. The manufacturer itself is transparent about this limitation and explicitly markets Drylok Concrete Protector for above-grade masonry rather than below-grade or heavily pressurized applications — for genuine below-grade hydrostatic protection, Drylok's own Extreme masonry waterproofer or a penetrating silane-siloxane product is the more appropriate choice. Used within its intended scope — above-grade retaining walls, garden walls, exterior block foundations without sustained water table pressure — it is a genuinely effective and extremely accessible waterproofer. Coverage runs 75-100 square feet per gallon on porous block, requiring roughly 1.5 to 2 gallons per 100 square feet of typical CMU wall, at a budget-friendly $35-45 per gallon.",
