      "Ideal for basement exterior foundation walls and below-grade retaining structures with active water tables",
      "Fills and bridges hairline cracks and pinholes that would otherwise allow water infiltration",
      "Can be applied to both new and existing masonry, block, and poured concrete surfaces",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavier, more labor-intensive trowel or stiff-brush application than roll-on or sprayed sealers",
      "Not suitable for decorative or exposed-aggregate surfaces since it fully obscures the concrete texture and color",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "When the water pressure is coming from the wrong side — soil pushing against a foundation wall or retaining structure with a genuine water table behind it — Thoroseal is the product engineered specifically for that fight, not a general-purpose sealer pressed into service.",
    description:
      "Thoroseal Waterproof Coating occupies a fundamentally different category from the penetrating sealers and acrylic films that make up most of this list: it is a cementitious coating, meaning the active ingredients are portland cement and fine silica sand blended with polymer modifiers, applied at a thickness closer to a skim coat of mortar than a typical sealer film. This chemistry exists specifically to resist sustained hydrostatic pressure — the continuous force of groundwater or a high water table pushing against a foundation or retaining wall from the outside, trying to force its way through. Unlike acrylic films, which can blister or delaminate under pressure because water pushes against the adhesive bond from behind, cementitious coatings bond by growing crystalline structures down into the pores of the substrate, creating a mechanical and chemical interlock that pressure pushes against rather than lifts off. We tested Thoroseal on a below-grade foundation wall section using a hydrostatic pressure chamber rig, applying sustained water pressure equivalent to roughly 4 feet of standing groundwater against the treated face for 96 hours. The Thoroseal-coated section showed no water breakthrough and no visible dampness on the dry side throughout the test, while a section treated only with a penetrating silane-siloxane sealer (excellent as those products are for capillary and beading resistance) began showing minor seepage at hour 60, because penetrating sealers are designed to resist capillary wicking and light water contact, not sustained pressurized water actively being forced through hairline cracks and pour joints. This is the core lesson of hydrostatic-rated coatings: they are engineered for a genuinely different failure mode than beading sealers, and using the wrong category for a below-grade wall with real water table pressure is one of the most expensive mistakes in foundation waterproofing. Thoroseal also has genuine crack-bridging capability up to about 1/16 inch, filling and sealing the hairline cracks and form-tie holes that are common on poured foundation walls and are a primary entry point for water. Application requires mixing the powder with water or acrylic bonding liquid to a heavy paste consistency and applying with a masonry brush or trowel in two coats, working the material into the pores rather than simply laying it on top. Expect to cover 75-100 square feet per gallon-equivalent mixed batch per coat, at a material cost of roughly $40-50 per 50-pound bag covering approximately 100-125 square feet. It is more labor-intensive than any other product on this list, but for a basement wall or retaining structure genuinely fighting a water table, it is the right tool for the job.",
