          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will sealer stop water coming through a retaining wall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A quality penetrating sealer applied to the exposed face of a retaining wall will significantly reduce water infiltration and stop efflorescence caused by capillary wicking, but it cannot fully substitute for proper drainage. Retaining walls should always have drainage gravel and a perforated drain pipe behind them to relieve hydrostatic pressure; the sealer is a second line of defense against the water that reaches the wall face, not a replacement for managing where the water goes.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I test if a sealer is actually waterproofing my concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Sprinkle a tablespoon of water on the treated surface. Genuinely waterproofed concrete will cause the water to form a tight, rounded bead that sits on top of the surface rather than spreading out or soaking in. If the water darkens the concrete or absorbs within a minute or two, the sealer has either failed, worn off, or was never a true waterproofing chemistry to begin with. This simple bead test is the same basic principle labs use with a goniometer to measure contact angle.",
          },
